build_windows_jni.sh always asks vcvarsall for the amd64 tools, so on an
ARM64 Windows the JNI DLL comes out as AMD64 and the ARM64 JVM refuses it:

	windows_jni.dll: Can't load AMD 64-bit .dll on a ARM 64-bit platform

The first attempt at this wrote the test into the generated batch file, as
%PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE%=="ARM64". That is the one variable that cannot be
believed here. run 35898166974 measured it: the shell saw ARM64 and applied
this patch, and the batch file still chose amd64, because

	PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE=AMD64
	PROCESSOR_ARCHITEW6432=(none)
	uname -m: x86_64  uname -s: MINGW64_NT-10.0-26200-ARM64

The MSYS shell is an x86_64 build running under emulation, so it and the
environment it inherits both report x86_64. uname -s carries the real one.

Decide it in the shell, where that is readable, and write the answer into
the batch file rather than a test.  CI only for now.

--- a/scripts/bootstrap/build_windows_jni.sh
+++ b/scripts/bootstrap/build_windows_jni.sh
@@ -114,9 +114,19 @@
 # directory. See https://github.com/bazelbuild/bazel/issues/3906
 abs_pwd="$(cygpath -a -w "${PWD}")"
 pwd_drive="$(echo "$abs_pwd" | head -c2)"
+# Pick the toolchain for the machine this is running on.  Neither of the two
+# obvious tests works on an ARM64 Windows: %PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE% reads
+# AMD64 because the MSYS shell is an x86_64 build running under emulation,
+# and uname -m says x86_64 for the same reason.  uname -s carries the real
+# one, as MINGW64_NT-10.0-26200-ARM64.
+case "$(uname -s)" in
+*ARM64*|*arm64*) vs_arch=arm64 ;;
+*)               vs_arch=amd64 ;;
+esac
+
 cat > "${VSTEMP}/windows_jni.bat" <<EOF
 @echo OFF
-@call "${VSVARS}" amd64
+@call "${VSVARS}" ${vs_arch}
 @$pwd_drive
 @cd "$abs_pwd"
 @set TMP=$(cygpath -a -w "${VSTEMP}")
